ent  we're going to see that we're going to  pass an additional flag that tells basil  to execute all of the build actions  remotely so this is setup that has been  done on this project there's a bit of  configuration and then you pass this  additional that doesn't work another - -  config equals remote flag and then basil  knows to run these actions remotely and  if we see here we can see that a hundred  and fifty actions are running  simultaneously so this project was  configured with a hundred and fifty  remote workers and basil is utilizing  all of it and this means that it's  running as many tests as the  configuration allows for so now what  happens is now that we see that the  number of actions running simultaneously  is dropping that's simply because there  aren't any more tests left to run and so  at this point you're simply bounded by  your slowest running test so let's just  go back at this point one hundred forty  two tests have been executed so far we  go back to the job that was running  locally we see that only 20 tests have  been executed and the times that we've  been working  yeah a couple of these tests take a  while to run so we will move on but we  see 100 154 have finished so far and  they're all concerning simultaneously so  14 are still running alright so that was  a demonstration of how basal will run in  the cloud and now I'm going to pass it  on to Alex to talk about angular and how  they've been using it thanks Donna hi  everybody my name is Alex eagle I work  on the angular team at Google I'm the  lead for our tooling team which includes  things like making the tools that our  angular users about one and a half  million of them used to develop their  applications as well as supporting our  own team and our internal development  velocity and I'm gonna tell you about  how much basil has improved our lives if  you haven't heard of angular it's a  enterprise scale web framework for  building very modern web applications  and it is a big project so how big is it  so as Donna pointed out the development  velocity on your project it depends on  how many people and how big the tests  are and you might think that you might  would need to break up your project so  you don't run all the tests all the time  that's really not what we wanted to do  so we wanted to use basil so that we  could run all of our tests all the time  and keep our development velocity high  and our numbers I just grabbed these off  of github and in the last 30 days we've  had 92 authors over 400 commits touching  over a thousand files so this is I would  say a pretty medium scale I know a lot  of enterprises have much bigger apps  than this but it is enough of a scale  that we saw the problem the basil solves  so this is what our continuous  integration time looks like you can  imagine a developer on the angular team  one of those 95 people having to wait an  hour for all of the tests to run and  then they find out something was broken  and so you can imagine going through  this round-trip many times this  significantly hurt our development  velocity and I want to point out here  that the numbers I'm showing here are  non incremental so this is just like the  uncashed  that's sort of the detail of the fact  that we get contributions from the open  source community and so using our own  cash has some security risks for us and  so this graph is showing that 60 minutes  is before we enabled the remote build  execution the dash dash config equals  remote sadhana showed and today I just  checked this morning and a typical  round-trip time is seven and a half  minutes to run all of the tests so this  is a decrease of 87% on our test time  and it has made a really big difference  to the angular team there are other  features of basil that that have helped  us out a lot  this is this is you know he's easily  worth the investment that we put into  migrating our build to basil so this  talk is about developing like Google and  the angular project 